themer - suckless-style theme manager

One theme, one dark/light switch, applied to the whole desktop: GTK, Qt, Firefox,
sway or i3, waybar, foot, nvim, fcitx5, launchers, newt dialogs, the TTY. Every
change is recorded and can be undone.

Build:    make              (also builds themer-gtk when pkg-config finds gtk+-3.0,
                             themer-qt when it finds Qt6Widgets or Qt5Widgets and a C++
                             compiler, and the Qt platform theme plugin for each Qt
                             major found; prints what it found; GTK=0 or QT=0 skips)
Check:    make check        (renders every template, then an apply/unload round trip
                             in a throwaway HOME; see test/run.sh)
          FIREFOX=/path/to/firefox sh test/firefox.sh [theme] [dark|light]
                            (renders the chrome in a real Firefox under Xvfb and writes
                             screenshots and the toolbar geometry to test/firefox/out/)
Install:  sudo make install (removes everything an earlier install put under PREFIX
                             first, so nothing stale stays; binaries to PREFIX/bin, themes and templates to
                             PREFIX/share/themer, modules to PREFIX/lib/themer/modules,
                             the module header to PREFIX/include/themer)
          sudo make uninstall  removes all of that; ~/.config/themer is yours and stays
Package:  dist/PKGBUILD and dist/themer.install for Arch: "make dist" writes
          themer-VERSION.tar.gz from the git tree, then makepkg -si next to it. The
          Qt platform theme plugin is built against the installed Qt and must be
          rebuilt after a Qt minor upgrade; a package makes that one command.
Version:  themer -v. VERSION is set at the top of the Makefile.

Status:   0.1. Proven on sway (Wayland) with GTK 3.24 and 4, Qt 5.15 and 6, Firefox
          140, fcitx5, waybar, foot; the i3/X11 paths run in make check but have had
          less time on a real X session. "set import" is a converter of best effort:
          an imported theme is a starting point to edit, not a finished set.

Layout:
  src/        the host: one binary. main.c is the command line; state.c the state
              file; registry.c finds modules and runs apply; manifest.c the tm_*
              helpers modules write with, and the undo log; template.c and palette.c
              the templates and the shared variables; theme.c the theme files and
              readability rules; targets.c the env, tty, and grub targets; tui.c,
              fonts.c, preview.c the interactive parts
  gui/        themer-gtk (GTK 3, C) and themer-qt (Qt 5 or 6, C++), the graphical
              frontends; common.c is what they share; qpa/ the Qt platform theme
              plugin that themes Qt programs without qt5ct/qt6ct
  include/    themer_module.h, the API modules are built against
  modules/    one .c per module; wm.h, launcher.h, icons.h are shared code
  templates/  templates/<set>/<dir>/<file>; a set is one directory (themer_chicago is
              the built-in), <dir> is named by the module (several can share one)
  themes/     <name>.theme, a [dark] and a [light] section each
  icons/      Adwaita's symbolic icons, the fallback for the Firefox toolbar
  test/       make check

Usage:
  themer                     TUI
  themer-gtk | themer-qt [PAGE]  the same settings in a GTK or Qt window, with a live
                             preview; PAGE opens on theme, fonts, icons, cursor, or modules
  themer apply               re-apply everything and save state (run at login)
  themer toggle              flip dark/light and apply
  themer dark | light        set mode and apply
  themer theme NAME          switch theme and apply
  themer list                list themes
  themer check               list every color the readability rules adjust
  themer env NAME=VALUE      set an environment variable and apply
  themer icons [NAME]        show (current first) or set the icon theme and apply
  themer cursor [NAME [SIZE]] show or set the cursor theme and size (pixels) and apply
  themer font ui|mono FAMILY [SIZE]  set the interface or terminal font and apply
  themer render [hinting none|slight|medium|full] [antialias on|off]
               [rgba none|rgb|bgr|vrgb|vbgr]   show or set the font rendering and apply
  themer set                 show the template set
  themer set NAME            switch every module to template set NAME and apply
  themer set list            list the sets and the modules each covers
  themer set copy FROM TO    copy a set under a new name, to edit
  themer module list         list modules, on/off, description
  themer module load NAME    load a module now (writes its files)
  themer module unload NAME  unload a module now (removes what it wrote)
  themer module options NAME          list a module's options
  themer module option NAME KEY VALUE set one; a loaded module reloads at once
  themer template list DIR [SET]      list template sets, or the files in SET;
                                      DIR may be a module name
  themer template render MODULE SET FILE [OUT]
                             render one template file with the current theme, to
                             stdout or OUT, for copying to another machine

Lookup, most specific first:
  themes:    $THEMER_THEMES, ~/.config/themer/themes, PREFIX/share/themer/themes
  modules:   $THEMER_MODULES, ~/.config/themer/modules, PREFIX/lib/themer/modules
  templates: ~/.config/themer/templates, $THEMER_TEMPLATES, PREFIX/share/themer/templates
             (the built-in sets are mirrored into the first; see TEMPLATES)
  icons:     $THEMER_ICONS, PREFIX/share/themer/icons (the fallback only; see Firefox)
  Running from the source tree:
      THEMER_THEMES=$PWD/themes THEMER_MODULES=$PWD/modules THEMER_TEMPLATES=$PWD/templates \
      THEMER_ICONS=$PWD/icons ./themer

State: ~/.config/themer/state (theme, mode, template set, fonts, icon theme, cursor
theme and size, font rendering, environment, which modules are on, option values). Compile-time defaults are in config.def.h.


GUI

themer-gtk and themer-qt are the TUI as a window, like nwg-look: pages for the theme,
the fonts, the icon theme, the cursor, and the modules on the left, a preview on the
right, Apply at the bottom, and File / Help menus (Apply is Ctrl+S, Revert to applied
undoes browsing). The preview is the real thing, not a mock-up: the window restyles
itself from the gtk.css (or qt.qss and palette) the gtk (or qt) module would write for
the selected theme, so what the widgets look like there is what every GTK (or Qt) app
will look like after Apply. The Icons page shows the selected icon theme's own icons,
the Cursor page draws the cursor theme's own files at the chosen size, the Terminal
tab the terminal font and colors, and the swatches the roles and the sixteen terminal
colors. Theme rows carry a swatch of the theme in the current mode. Nothing is
written until Apply, which runs the same apply as 'themer apply': the env, tty, and
grub targets and every module that is on, so terminals, GTK, Qt, Firefox, and the
rest all change together. Closing with unapplied changes asks first.

Template sets from the window: on the Theme page, "New set" copies the selected
set under a new name into ~/.config/themer/templates/ (to edit), "Import theme"
turns a theme you already have into part of a set (a GTK theme directory, a .qss,
a chrome/ directory...; see WRITING A TEMPLATE SET), "Check" renders the selected
set for every theme and shows the report. The TUI has the same under Template set
(n, i, c). Module options are on the Modules page: open a module (GTK) or select
it (Qt) for its options, which take effect at once, the same as the TUI's module
menu and themer module option; "template" pins one module to a set of its own.

They need the host to be built: make builds them when pkg-config finds gtk+-3.0
(themer-gtk) or Qt6Widgets / Qt5Widgets and a C++ compiler (themer-qt), and skips
them otherwise, saying so; 'make gui' repeats what was found, 'make themer-gtk' or
'make themer-qt' builds one on purpose. themer-qt needs no moc.


WHAT APPLY COVERS (the ground nwg-look and qt5ct/qt6ct cover, from one state)

Whichever front end changed the settings (CLI, TUI, themer-gtk, themer-qt), Apply
writes the same files:
  gtk module    ~/.config/gtk-3.0/settings.ini and gtk-4.0/settings.ini: theme, icon
                theme, cursor theme and size, font, prefer-dark, Xft antialias/
                hinting/hintstyle/rgba; the same keys in ~/.gtkrc-2.0 (GTK 2) and
                ~/.config/xsettingsd/xsettingsd.conf (xsettingsd gets SIGHUP); the
                gsettings keys of org.gnome.desktop.interface (gtk-theme, icon-theme,
                cursor-theme, cursor-size, font-name, monospace-font-name,
                color-scheme, font-antialiasing, font-hinting, font-rgba-order) when
                gsettings is installed (option gsettings=off turns that off)
  qt module     ~/.config/themer/qt/theme.conf and themer.qss for themer's platform
                theme plugin (style, palette, icon theme, fonts, stylesheet), and
                qt5ct.conf / qt6ct.conf with the same where those tools are
                installed. qt5ct's Interface page (dialogs, wheel lines,
                double-click...) is your own and is left as it is
  cursor module ~/.icons/default/index.theme and ~/.local/share/icons/default/
                index.theme (Inherits=), XCURSOR_THEME and XCURSOR_SIZE in
                environment.d, Xcursor.theme/size in ~/.Xresources (merged with xrdb
                when an X server runs); sway gets 'seat * xcursor_theme' from the sway
                module, i3 reads the Xresources
  fontconfig module  ~/.config/fontconfig/conf.d/50-themer-rendering.conf with the
                antialias, hinting, hintstyle, rgba, and lcdfilter settings, for
                everything that is not GTK
Icon and cursor themes are listed from ~/.icons, ~/.local/share/icons,
/usr/share/icons, and /usr/local/share/icons; a cursor theme is one with a cursors/
directory. Any name can also be typed.

QT

  Qt programs take a palette, fonts, an icon theme, and a style only from a
  "platform theme" plugin, which is why qt5ct and qt6ct exist. themer brings its
  own: gui/qpa/themerqpa.cpp, built once per Qt major that make finds (Qt 5 and
  Qt 6 development files; make says which) and installed under
  PREFIX/lib/themer/qt/qt5/ and qt6/. The qt module writes ~/.config/themer/qt/
  theme.conf (style, icon theme, fonts, the palette) and themer.qss, and sets
  QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=themer and QT_PLUGIN_PATH in its environment file. The
  plugin watches those files, so an apply restyles running Qt programs at once.
  Where the plugin is not installed for your Qt, the module falls back to qt5ct
  (and qt6ct, which answers to the same name) when they are installed; the
  platformtheme option forces either. Qt reads the environment at start: see
  ENVIRONMENT for how the variables reach your programs.


ENVIRONMENT

  Several things are environment variables: QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME and
  QT_PLUGIN_PATH,
  XCURSOR_THEME and XCURSOR_SIZE, the fcitx input-method variables, and whatever
  you set with themer env. themer writes them to ~/.config/environment.d/*themer*.conf,
  which reaches programs when the session is started through systemd (a display
  manager, or sway as a user service). When you start the compositor yourself from
  a tty, nothing reads environment.d: then source the script themer also writes,
  from your shell profile, before the compositor starts:
      . ~/.config/themer/env.sh        (in ~/.profile, ~/.zprofile, or ~/.bash_profile)
  It exports every variable from those files. Check with  echo $QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME
  in a terminal inside the session: empty means the variables are not reaching your
  programs, and Qt will look plain however the rest is set up. /etc/environment is
  not involved (themer never writes system files for this).


MODULES (on by default unless noted)

  Detection: a module says whether the program it themes is here (present() in the
  module API: the binary on PATH, a config file, a profile, a socket). An absent one
  is listed as "absent" by 'themer module list', shown as "(not installed)" in the
  TUI and the GUIs, and skipped by apply; it stays on, so it takes effect as soon as
  the program appears. Modules without a check (gtk, cursor, fontconfig, newt, st)
  always write; their files are harmless where the program is missing.

  gtk      (restarts the GTK file-chooser portal on apply, so Firefox's file dialogs
           on Wayland, which come from it, follow the theme without a logout)
           a GTK 3/4 theme written to ~/.config/themer/gtk/ (like qt's files), reached
           by GTK through a link ~/.themes/<set> -> there, named after the template
           set (themer_chicago, labelled "Themer Chicago"; gtk-theme-name follows); no
           GTK_THEME is set, since that would override the file and go stale);
           options gtk2, gtk3, gtk4 turn each version off for systems without it;
           the settings.ini files, ~/.gtkrc-2.0, xsettingsd.conf, and gsettings carry
           the theme, icons, cursor, font, and rendering (see WHAT APPLY COVERS).
           A GTK app keeps the theme it loaded at launch: restart it, or log out.
  qt       Qt 5 and 6: palette, flat stylesheet, icons, fonts, through themer's own
           platform theme plugin (see QT), so qt5ct/qt6ct are not needed; where they
           are installed their configs are written too. Options qt5 and qt6 turn
           either version off; platformtheme picks the plugin or qt5ct.
  cursor   the mouse cursor theme and size: Xcursor default theme, XCURSOR_* env,
           Xresources
  fontconfig  the font rendering settings for everything that reads fontconfig
  firefox  the browser chrome (chrome/userChrome.css) and Firefox's own pages such as
           settings (chrome/userContent.css), as marked blocks in the profile (see Firefox)
  thunderbird  the mail window (tabs, toolbars, the spaces bar, menus, compose) and the
           panes and pages Thunderbird draws as content (folders, the message list, the
           header, settings, address book, calendar), the same way (see Thunderbird)
  sway     window and bar colors, the font, $menu, the seat cursor; loads only where
           sway is in use. Borders, gaps, and titlebars stay in your own config.
  i3       the same for i3
  waybar   dwm-style bar; owns style.css, the original is kept as style.css.themer-orig
           and put back on unload. config.jsonc is not touched.
  foot     font and colors in foot.ini ([colors-dark] and [colors-light], which foot
           1.15 and newer accept, plus initial-color-theme)
  nvim     a 'themer' colorscheme, selected from the end of init.lua or init.vim
  fcitx    fcitx5 input-method variables and candidate-window theme, only if installed
  wmenu    a launcher script, ~/.config/themer/bin/themer-wmenu, in the theme's colors
  dmenu    the same for dmenu, themer-dmenu
  newt     NEWT_COLORS for nmtui and other newt dialogs, each color mapped to the
           nearest of the theme's terminal colors. Set in environment.d, so it reaches
           programs started from the systemd user session; copy the line to your
           shell profile for the others.
  st       (off) a colors header; st's config.h needs one edit, see modules/st.c
  example  (off) the template module to copy

  Built-in targets, not modules: env (environment.d/50-themer.conf), tty (the
  console palette and font, as root), grub (as root). They write system files the
  undo log cannot cover.

How modules change files:
  Every write goes through the tm_* helpers in themer_module.h and is recorded in
  ~/.local/state/themer/<module>.manifest. Unloading reverses the records: generated
  files are deleted (only while still generated by themer), replaced files are put
  back from their .themer-orig copy, marked blocks are cut out, ini keys and replaced
  lines get their old values, and directories themer created are removed when empty.
  Applying runs unload-then-load, so re-applying never duplicates anything.
  After a change, each module's reload hook tells its running program: sway and i3
  are reloaded when their socket variable is set, and running waybar gets SIGUSR2.

Module options:
  Set them in the TUI (module menu, then o) or with themer module option. Modules
  that render templates all have "template", the set name. Unset, it follows the
  global template set (themer set NAME, or Template set in the TUI); set on a module,
  it pins that module to a set of its own.
      gtk      font     ui | terminal          (default ui)
               gtk2, gtk3, gtk4   on | off      (default on: each GTK version themed)
      qt       font     ui | terminal          (default ui)
               qt5, qt6 on | off               (default on: each Qt version themed)
               platformtheme auto | themer | qt5ct  (default auto: the plugin when installed)
      firefox  font     ui | terminal          (default ui)
      firefox  icons    0-8, the glyph size in points above the text (default 2)
      firefox  style    image | mask           (default image: the theme's icons, recolored;
                                                mask: every icon's shape in the bar ink)
      firefox  recolor  all | symbolic         (default all: every SVG icon in the bar ink)
      firefox  raster   color | ink            (default color: PNG icons keep their colors)
      thunderbird font  ui | terminal          (default ui)
      waybar   font     terminal | ui          (default terminal)
      waybar   padding  tight | normal | loose (default normal)
      fcitx    font     ui | terminal          (default ui)
      wmenu    font     ui | terminal          (default ui)
      dmenu    font     ui | terminal          (default ui)
      sway, i3 font     ui | terminal          (default ui)
               menu     auto | wmenu | dmenu | none  (default auto)
               bar      auto | on | off        (default auto: a bar only if the config has none)
      example  style    short | long


TEMPLATES

  A module's files come from templates. A set is one directory,
  ~/.config/themer/templates/<set>/, holding one subdirectory per module (modules
  with the same output share one) and one file per output:

      gtk/       gtk.css                       (GTK 3 and 4)
      qt/        qt.qss, colors.conf           (qt5ct and qt6ct)
      firefox/   userChrome.css, userContent.css
      thunderbird/ tokens.css, chrome.css, content.css  (tokens + chrome -> userChrome.css,
                 tokens + content -> userContent.css)
      wm/        themer.conf, bar.conf         (sway and i3)
      launcher/  launcher                      (wmenu and dmenu)
      waybar/    style.css
      nvim/      themer.lua, select.lua, select.vim
      fcitx/     theme.conf
      newt/      newt.conf
      st/        colors.h
      example/   colors

  The built-in set is themer_chicago. It is
  installed under PREFIX/share/themer/templates, and every start of themer mirrors it
  into ~/.config/themer/templates/themer_chicago/, which is the copy that is used:
  edit it there. The mirror keeps itself current without losing your edits: a file
  you have not touched follows the built-in when "make install" brings a new one; a
  file you changed is yours and stays (themer says so once if the built-in moved on,
  naming the new one under PREFIX for you to compare); a file you delete comes back.
  How it tells the two apart is .seeded in that directory, a hash of each file as
  themer last wrote it. A copy left by an old build (none in .seeded) that differs
  is replaced and kept beside as <file>.themer-old. A mirrored file whose built-in
  is gone after an upgrade is removed again, unless you changed it. Your own sets live next to it,
  ~/.config/themer/templates/<set>/, with names like set names: letters, digits,
  '-', '_'. $THEMER_TEMPLATES stands in for the system directory (a source tree).

  Switching sets: "themer set NAME" (or Template set in the TUI) makes every module
  render from NAME and applies. A set need not cover every module: what it lacks
  comes from themer_chicago, so a set that restyles only Firefox still applies to
  the whole desktop ("themer set list" shows what each set covers). One module can be
  pinned to another set with its template option.

  To work on the look, edit ~/.config/themer/templates/themer_chicago/ directly and
  apply. For a second variant, copy a set under a new name and change that:
      themer set copy themer_chicago mine
      (edit ~/.config/themer/templates/mine/), then: themer set mine
  That copies every module directory the source set has; delete the ones you do not
  want to change under mine, and themer_chicago's files are used there.

  Usage:
      themer set import NAME MODULE PATH   turn an existing theme into (part of) a set
      themer set check NAME                render every file of NAME for every theme
      themer set list                      the sets, what each covers, its description


WRITING A TEMPLATE SET (converting a theme you already have)

  A set is a directory of ordinary theme files with {{variables}} in them. Nothing
  else: no build, no manifest format to learn, and a set may cover one module or
  all of them. The quickest start is a theme you already use:

      themer set import mylook gtk ~/.themes/Gruvbox-Material      (a GTK theme dir)
      themer set import mylook qt ~/.config/qt5ct/qss/mine.qss     (a Qt stylesheet)
      themer set import mylook firefox ~/.mozilla/firefox/x.default/chrome
      themer set import mylook waybar ~/.config/waybar/style.css
      themer set check mylook
      themer set mylook

  Import takes a file or a directory. It knows the usual layouts (a GTK theme's
  gtk-3.0/ and gtk-4.0/, a profile's chrome/) and otherwise takes the directory as
  it is. Each text file goes under the name the module renders (gtk.css, qt.qss,
  userChrome.css...; themer template list MODULE shows them), with a "generated
  by themer" marker line added at the top, and every color in it that equals one
  of the current theme's colors is replaced by that color's variable: #282828 in a
  file imported under gruvbox dark becomes #{{bg}}, #83a598 becomes #{{acc}}. So
  import with the theme and mode the existing look was made for, and the file
  follows every theme from then on. Colors that match nothing stay literal and are
  listed in the report; change those to variables by hand where they should move
  with the theme. Everything that is not text (images, fonts, whole subdirectories
  such as assets/) is copied as it is and placed beside the module's output on
  apply, so url("assets/x.png") keeps working. A file whose name the module does
  not render and that is not an asset is refused with the list of names to use.

  What each module directory takes, and what becomes of it on apply:

    gtk/        gtk.css          rendered to ~/.config/themer/gtk/gtk-3.0/gtk.css and
                                 gtk-4.0/gtk.css (one stylesheet for both)
                gtk4.css         optional: GTK 4's gtk.css instead of the shared one
                gtk-dark.css     optional: what GTK 3 loads in dark mode, as any theme
                any other .css   rendered into both directories under its name
                anything else    copied into both directories (assets/, images)
                GTK 3 selects the theme through settings.ini; GTK 4 imports
                gtk-4.0/gtk.css from a block in ~/.config/gtk-4.0/gtk.css. The set's
                gtk.css is the whole theme: there is no Adwaita underneath it.
    qt/         qt.qss           the stylesheet, to ~/.config/themer/qt/themer.qss (and
                                 qt5ct/qt6ct's copies). {{qt_assets}} is the directory it
                                 lands in: url({{qt_assets}}/x.svg) for its images, since a
                                 Qt stylesheet's url() is not relative to the stylesheet
                *.svg            rendered like templates (arrows, check marks in the
                                 theme's colors) and written beside the stylesheet;
                                 anything else copied beside it as it is
                colors.conf      the qt5ct/qt6ct palette; the module supplies
                                 {{qt_active}}, {{qt_disabled}}, {{qt_inactive}}, the
                                 three role lists built from the theme (keep the
                                 built-in file unless you want other mappings)
    firefox/    userChrome.css   a marked block at the end of the profile's
                userContent.css  chrome/userChrome.css and userContent.css; other
                                 files copied into chrome/ (url() resolves there).
                                 Module variables: {{tab_font}}, {{tab_size}},
                                 {{glyph_size}}, and per toolbar icon {{icon_X}},
                                 {{icon_X_hover}}, {{raster_X}}, {{tint_X}},
                                 {{tint_X_hover}} (X: back forward reload stop home
                                 newtab menu downloads extensions star_off star
                                 reader identity close), data: URIs of the icon theme
    thunderbird/ tokens.css, chrome.css, content.css   tokens+chrome -> userChrome,
                                 tokens+content -> userContent; an imported
                                 userChrome.css/userContent.css become chrome.css/
                                 content.css with an empty tokens.css. {{font_family}},
                                 {{font_size}}
    wm/         themer.conf      included from the sway/i3 config: colors, font, the
                                 cursor; bar.conf the bar block. {{font_family}},
                                 {{font_size}}, {{bar_block}}, {{cursor_block}}
    launcher/   launcher         the wmenu/dmenu wrapper script. {{menu}}, {{args}}
    waybar/     style.css        replaces waybar's style.css (original kept aside).
                                 {{font_family}}, {{font_size}}, {{ws_pad}}, {{mod_pad}}
    nvim/       themer.lua       the colorscheme; select.lua / select.vim the line
                                 that selects it
    fcitx/      theme.conf       the fcitx5 classic UI theme. {{font_family}}, {{font_size}}
    newt/       newt.conf        NEWT_COLORS. {{newt_colors}}
    st/         colors.h         st's colorname[] header
    set.conf    name=, description=, author=, url= for themer set list (optional)

  Every file sees the shared variables below (roles, tones, fonts, ansi colors,
  cursor, rendering, padding) plus its module's own, listed above. Any file a set
  lacks comes from themer_chicago, so a set that covers gtk/ alone is complete.
  Rendered text files must start with the marker (import adds it): that is how
  themer tells its own files from yours when it overwrites and removes them.

  To share a set, give people the directory; it goes under
  ~/.config/themer/templates/<name>/ (or PREFIX/share/themer/templates/ for a
  package) and shows up in themer set list.

  A file contains {{name}} placeholders. The names every template can use:
      roles      bg fg surf acc accfg urg warn line on_urg on_warn
      tones      face light shade pressed field hover_bg hover_fg disabled bar input
                 bar_text input_text tab_text tab_active_text tab_hover_text hover_text
                 glyph_hover                                   (see Design language)
      terminal   ansi0 .. ansi15
      other      mode (dark or light), theme, icons, cursor, cursor_size, hinting,
                 antialias (1 or 0), rgba, ui_font, ui_size, mono_font, mono_size,
                 tty_font, pad_control, pad_field, pad_cell, pad_surface, pad_tip
  A module adds names of its own with TM_VARS: thunderbird has font_family, font_size;
  wm has font_family, font_size, bar_block, cursor_block; launcher has menu, args; qt has qt_active,
  qt_disabled, qt_inactive; firefox has tab_font, tab_size, glyph_size and the icon
  variables; waybar, fcitx, newt, and example have their own (see the module's source).
  An unknown name is an error, reported on apply, so a typo cannot reach the output.


DESIGN LANGUAGE (themer_chicago)

  Every module draws from the same roles (listed in themer_module.h), so GTK, Qt,
  Firefox, sway/i3, and waybar look like one design:
      bg, fg         windows, bars, menus; text on them
      surf           controls, inactive tabs, fields in the bar
      acc, accfg     the selected tab, focus rings, progress; text on acc
      urg, warn      urgent windows and tabs; modes and warnings (text: on_urg, on_warn)
      line           every 1px outline; outlines are square, never rounded
  The tones are derived from the roles in src/palette.c:
      light, shade   the Chicago bevel: raised controls lit at top and left, shaded at
                     bottom and right; sunken fields the other way round
      hover_bg, hover_fg  hovered controls and tabs, list and menu selection: the
                     accent when it reads 3:1 on the background, otherwise the
                     background moved toward the text until it does
      bar, input     the browser bar, and its search field a step lighter
      pad_*          control, field, cell, surface, and tip padding, from one scale:
                     4px 12px for buttons, tabs, and menu items; 4px 6px for fields;
                     3px 6px for list cells; 2px 0 for menus; 4px 8px for tooltips
  The GTK stylesheet is the most complete form of the look. Tabs look like sway/i3
  tabs in GTK notebooks, Firefox, and sway's tabbed layout. The sway/i3 template
  exports the tones as $chicago_* variables.


READABILITY

  Every theme is made readable when it is loaded, for both modes: text reads at 4.5:1
  on the surface it sits on, the selected-text color reads on the accent, and the
  urgent, warning, and terminal colors read at 4.5:1 on the background. A color that
  fails is moved toward the theme's own text color; black or white replaces it only
  where no move works. "themer check" lists every color that is adjusted.
  Text on the accent, the urgent color, and the warning color is chosen per color
  (accfg, on_urg, on_warn), since one text color cannot read on all three. Labels
  inside a highlighted button, tab, or menu item take the highlight's text color.


FIREFOX

  The chrome is three stacked rows at the top of the window, all the same height: the
  tabs, the navigation buttons with the URL bar, and the bookmarks. No rounded corners
  or shadows. Tabs are raised controls with the Chicago bevel, flush in one row, like
  GTK notebook tabs: the selected tab takes the accent (sunken), the others sit on the
  surface color, hover is the hover color, a tab that needs attention takes the urgent
  color, and a loading tab the warning color. The URL bar is a sunken field in the
  terminal font, the accent outline when focused; it stays in its row (no breaking
  out), and its suggestions hang from it in the same field, selection in the hover
  colors. Toolbar buttons are as tall as their row and center their icons, with no
  hover box: the hover is the icon's ink alone. Keyboard focus is a 1px accent outline.
  Popups (downloads, app menu, context menus), the Library, dialogs, and the sidebar
  are flat and outlined, with hover and disabled states. The template is written once,
  top to bottom, with Firefox's own variables set first so its default rules follow
  the theme too.

  Firefox's own pages (settings, add-ons, downloads, the new tab, every about: page)
  are not reached by userChrome.css; userContent.css covers them. It sets Firefox's
  in-content variables (page, boxes, buttons, links, focus, selection) to the theme's
  roles and gives buttons and fields the bevel, so the default blue and purple are
  gone there too. It also covers file:// directory listings and the PDF viewer
  (pdf.js's own variables). The GTK file chooser is GTK's: the gtk module themes it.
  The search-engine switcher in the URL bar is hidden; the engine is set in settings.

  The profile written to is the one a running Firefox holds (its "lock" symlink names
  the PID), otherwise the one used most recently. The module prints which, and whether
  a restart is needed: userChrome.css is read at profile startup only. It needs
  toolkit.legacyUserProfileCustomizations.stylesheets = true in about:config; the
  module does not write user.js. Profiles are looked for in ~/.config/mozilla/firefox,
  ~/.mozilla/firefox, the snap, and the Flatpak.

  Icons: the back, forward, reload, stop, home, new tab, menu, bookmark, downloads,
  extensions, reader, security, and tab close buttons show icons from the installed
  icon theme (the "icons" setting), searched along its Inherits chain, then hicolor,
  then Adwaita, in ~/.icons, ~/.local/share/icons, /usr/share/icons, and
  /usr/local/share/icons. Ranking: a symbolic SVG, then another SVG, then a PNG. Icons
  are embedded in the stylesheet as list-style-image, the way Firefox's own icons are
  set, so Firefox needs no file access. By default each icon is the theme's own,
  recolored to the bar's ink (hover ink on hover); the module's style option set to
  mask draws every icon's shape as a CSS mask over the ink instead, in a fixed box,
  which evens out an icon theme with mixed artwork. A glyph missing from every theme
  in the chain comes from the bundled Adwaita icons (icons/, installed to
  PREFIX/share/themer/icons; $THEMER_ICONS overrides), so every button has an icon
  whatever the icon theme. The two sets differ only in their icon rules.

  Limit: a stylesheet cannot move Firefox's page-action buttons into the nav bar; use
  Customize Toolbar once to drag them there.

  How the template reaches Firefox's own rules: Firefox draws the chrome from design
  tokens (--toolbar-field-border-color-focus, --panel-background-color, --tab-*, and
  so on), declared on :root and, inside its custom elements, on :host in a shadow tree
  that userChrome.css cannot reach. The template declares every token on * with
  !important, which wins in both places, under both the names of Firefox 115-140 and
  those of the "Nova" design in 158. Only after that come the explicit rules.

  Checked with test/firefox.sh in Firefox 140 (ESR) and Firefox 158 (Zen's build,
  which keeps the stock URL bar, panels, and menus): three 27px rows at the default
  fonts, icons centered in them, the URL bar in its row with the suggestions under it,
  no focus ring but the accent border, the app menu and notification bars in the
  theme, and the settings page themed, in dark and light.


THUNDERBIRD

  Thunderbird (115 and newer) draws its interface from design tokens much like
  Firefox's: --layout-background-0..4 and --layout-color-* for the panes, --selected-
  item-color for selection, and sets for the sidebar, toolbars, tabs, the spaces bar,
  lists and message cards, buttons, panels, fields, and the in-content pages.
  templates/thunderbird/<set>/tokens.css sets them all to the theme's roles, on every
  element, so the shadow trees of Thunderbird's custom elements follow too. The folder
  pane, message list, and message header are content documents (about:3pane,
  about:message) since 115, so the tokens go into userContent.css as well, followed by
  content.css; the window gets tokens plus chrome.css in userChrome.css. Tabs are
  painted like Firefox's: the fill on .tab-content, the selected tab the accent.
  Mapping: content and lists on the field color; the center panes, sidebars, and
  windows on bg; toolbars, the spaces bar, and the message header on the bar color;
  blocks on surf; controls raised on face with the bevel; the selected folder, message,
  tab, and space the accent, the hover color elsewhere.

  The profile is found as for Firefox, in ~/.config/thunderbird, ~/.thunderbird, the
  snap, and the Flatpak.
  It needs toolkit.legacyUserProfileCustomizations.stylesheets = true in the config
  editor (Settings > General > Config Editor), and a restart after applying. Not
  checked by running Thunderbird here (no build reachable); the selectors and token
  names are taken from Thunderbird's own stylesheets (mail/themes/shared/mail).

THEMES

  Each file's header names its sources and says where values are approximate.
      habamax, crimson, desert, slate   neovim-derived
      catppuccin    Mocha (dark), Latte (light)
      gruvbox       dark and light
      rose-pine     main (dark), dawn (light)
      dracula       Dracula (dark), Alucard (light)
      nord          dark; the light section is derived
      tokyonight    night (dark), day (light)
      solarized     dark and light
      github        Primer default dark and light (terminal colors approximate)
      zenbones      zenwritten, dark and light
      xterm         xterm's own defaults, dark and light
      greyscale     neutral grays, high contrast
      suckless      dwm's and dmenu's default colors; ANSI approximate, light derived


FONTS

  The font browser lists every installed family, from fc-list when present and from
  the name table of each .ttf/.otf/.ttc under the standard font directories. The UI
  font goes to GTK, Qt, waybar, sway/i3, and fcitx; the terminal font to foot (also
  the top-level font= line of foot.ini) and the Qt fixed font. Ctrl-P in the font
  browser writes an HTML specimen in the theme's colors to ~/.cache/themer/fonts/ and
  opens it with xdg-open.


WRITING A MODULE

  See include/themer_module.h and modules/example.c. A module is one C file built
  with  cc -shared -fPIC -I PREFIX/include/themer -o name.so name.c  and dropped into
  ~/.config/themer/modules/. Modules are compiled against the layout of TmModule and
  TmTheme (TM_ABI); one built for another layout is skipped with a message. A module
  with a present() hook is skipped by apply where its program is missing. "make
  install" replaces the installed modules; rebuild any in ~/.config/themer/modules.

Upgrading:
  From a build that rendered the theme into ~/.config/gtk-3.0/gtk.css: the gtk module
  moves that file to gtk.css.themer-old on the next apply (a user stylesheet outranks
  the theme, so left there it would keep feeding its colors to programs that read the
  named colors); delete the copy once nothing is missed. From a build whose templates were ~/.config/themer/templates/<dir>/<set>/: the
  layout is <set>/<dir>/ now; the old directories are not read, delete them. A
  saved set name "Themer Chicago" is read as themer_chicago. From a
  build with the single "wm" module: run "themer module unload wm" with that
  build first, so its themer.conf and include line are removed. From a build whose
  waybar module used a marked block: "themer module unload waybar" with that build.
  A saved template option of "default" falls back to themer_chicago. Modules built
  before the cursor and rendering settings and the present() hook (TM_ABI 3 or 4)
  must be rebuilt (TM_ABI 5). The sway/i3 border, gaps, and titlebar options and the
  qt interface options are gone: set those in your own config; a saved value is ignored.
